1
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:15,540
Hi preppies, welcome back to our reading lesson.

2
00:00:16,020 --> 00:00:22,420
Today we're going to learn about the last question
type in the course, which is Yes, No or Not Given.

3
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:27,700
After many years of teaching, I have noticed that many students assume this

4
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:33,080
type and true, false or not given questions are the same, and they're just

5
00:00:33,080 --> 00:00:35,140
different in the way they are called.

6
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:40,900
This leads to the fact that candidates often write answers as true, false or

7
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:45,320
not given, to yes, no and not given questions, or vice versa.

8
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:49,860
This is an unnecessary mistake because even if you understand the passage

9
00:00:49,860 --> 00:00:55,220
correctly, but write the wrong answers compared to what is required, you still

10
00:00:55,220 --> 00:00:57,120
lose your points as usual.

11
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:02,620
So, what are the differences between these two question types that requires us

12
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:05,060
to identify two types of answers clearly?

13
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:11,140
To answer it, today we're going to explore yes, no or not given questions, then

14
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:14,620
compare them to the true, false or not given questions.

15
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:21,080
Three goals we need to achieve are Understand and identify some features of

16
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:23,140
yes, no or not given questions.

17
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:28,820
Understand and identify some features of three types of answers Introduction to

18
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:35,440
yes, no or not given questions Understand and apply some steps for yes, no or

19
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:40,380
not given questions Corresponding to three parts 1.

20
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:47,100
Introduction to yes, no or not given questions
and features of three types of answers 2.

21
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:51,900
Steps for yes, no or not given questions 3.

22
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:55,920
Practice Alright, no more delay, let's begin!

23
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:07,080
Here I have two examples of a true, false or not
given question and a yes, no or not given question.

24
00:02:07,699 --> 00:02:12,340
First of all, you have 30 seconds to look at both of them and identify

25
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:18,060
some similarities and differences between the two
question types based on the following criteria.

26
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:21,260
How many parts are there in the questions?

27
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:23,200
Where are requirements?

28
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:26,200
Where are statements which are numbered?

29
00:02:57,850 --> 00:03:03,910
Looking at these two question types, we can see that
they are quite similar to each other, aren't they?

30
00:03:04,410 --> 00:03:08,250
However, going into detail, we can still notice some differences.

31
00:03:09,150 --> 00:03:13,930
Firstly, in terms of different parts in the questions, they all have two parts.

32
00:03:14,790 --> 00:03:20,790
1. Requirements They require candidates
to decide if available statements are

33
00:03:20,790 --> 00:03:25,070
true, false or not given or yes, no or not given.

34
00:03:25,070 --> 00:03:32,210
2. Statements which are numbered In
the true, false or not given questions, the

35
00:03:32,210 --> 00:03:38,230
requirement is Do the following statements agree
with the information given in the reading passage?

36
00:03:38,610 --> 00:03:43,750
Meanwhile, for the yes, no or not given questions, it is Do the following

37
00:03:43,750 --> 00:03:48,950
statements agree with claims or views of the writer in the reading passage?

38
00:03:49,650 --> 00:03:54,230
Therefore, although both types require candidates to decide if the statements

39
00:03:54,230 --> 00:03:58,210
agree with the information in the passage, the difference is that for true,

40
00:03:58,530 --> 00:04:03,390
false or not given questions, the passage often includes facts, while yes, no

41
00:04:03,390 --> 00:04:06,610
or not given questions contain the writer's opinions.

42
00:04:07,110 --> 00:04:12,170
Regarding three types of answers, their features in yes, no or not given and

43
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:15,850
true, false or not given questions are quite similar to each other.

44
00:04:16,490 --> 00:04:21,870
They both require us to decide if the statements agree with, contradict, in

45
00:04:21,870 --> 00:04:26,910
other words disagree with, the information in the passage or are not mentioned.

46
00:04:27,550 --> 00:04:31,650
We should read the requirements carefully to avoid writing the answers as true,

47
00:04:31,930 --> 00:04:36,210
false or not given to yes, no or not given questions or vice versa.

48
00:04:36,810 --> 00:04:42,070
Besides the information above, the two
types also share one more thing in common.

49
00:04:42,650 --> 00:04:47,110
It is that the statements come in order as the information in the passage.

50
00:04:47,690 --> 00:04:52,990
Let's look at the summary table of the similarities
and differences between these two question types.

51
00:04:56,750 --> 00:05:05,570
From this table, we can see that the key difference between yes, no or not
given and true, false or not given questions is the type of information in the

52
00:05:05,570 --> 00:05:09,050
passage, leading to the difference between three types of answers.

53
00:05:09,850 --> 00:05:14,910
To be specific, the passages in the yes, no or not given questions usually show

54
00:05:14,910 --> 00:05:22,070
the writer's opinions on a problem, while the passages in true,
false or not given questions provide facts about the topic.

55
00:05:22,930 --> 00:05:28,110
A fact-based passage often includes truths and data, not personal opinions.

56
00:05:29,070 --> 00:05:34,090
Therefore, the information in the passage
is always correct and not controversial.

57
00:05:35,010 --> 00:05:41,970
In this type, facts are presented by the use of
statistics, research, surveys and specific names.

58
00:05:41,970 --> 00:05:48,810
On the other hand, an opinion-based passage shows
the writer's personal opinions, not the truth.

59
00:05:49,550 --> 00:05:52,570
So, there will be many different opinions on an issue.

60
00:05:53,590 --> 00:05:57,270
And the opinions are expressed by the use of pronouns.

61
00:05:57,730 --> 00:06:00,830
For example, I, we, you.

62
00:06:01,830 --> 00:06:09,190
Subjective language such as adjectives, adverbs,
comparison, contrast or qualifying words.

63
00:06:09,190 --> 00:06:16,050
Although there are some different features between fact-based and
opinion-based passages, the steps for them are actually similar.

64
00:06:16,730 --> 00:06:21,390
So, let's move on to part 2 to learn about
the steps for yes, no or not given questions.

65
00:06:25,970 --> 00:06:30,470
We will follow three main steps for yes, no or not given questions.

66
00:06:31,130 --> 00:06:36,110
Step 1. Underline keywords in statements
and pay attention to scanning words.

67
00:06:36,850 --> 00:06:40,730
Scanning words are keywords which are found more easily than other keywords,

68
00:06:40,730 --> 00:06:50,270
including unchangeable keywords like proper nouns,
acronyms and numbers, compounds and long words.

69
00:06:50,810 --> 00:06:56,790
Step 2. Scan passages to locate information
areas containing keywords and their paraphrases.

70
00:06:57,450 --> 00:07:04,030
Step 3. Read the information areas located in
step 2 carefully to identify correct answers.

71
00:07:04,470 --> 00:07:05,830
Here is a note for you.

72
00:07:05,830 --> 00:07:13,030
In the text, there are sentences where the writer may
not directly express their opinion, but rather imply it.

73
00:07:13,770 --> 00:07:17,790
Therefore, to identify the writer's opinion, we should pay attention to some

74
00:07:17,790 --> 00:07:23,430
indicators such as adjectives, adverbs, which are subjective language,

75
00:07:24,510 --> 00:07:29,590
qualifying words, compare and contrast and pronouns.

76
00:07:30,410 --> 00:07:33,270
Let's apply these three steps to the following reading passage.

77
00:07:33,270 --> 00:07:36,230
Now, let's put our knowledge to the test.

78
00:07:36,810 --> 00:07:41,170
We will use the three steps we just learned to solve the first question from

79
00:07:41,170 --> 00:07:43,270
a passage about global governance.

80
00:07:43,890 --> 00:07:45,350
Let's look at question 1.

81
00:07:45,870 --> 00:07:51,550
Global rules for environmental protection were
established before the rules for international finances.

82
00:07:52,950 --> 00:07:58,250
Step 1. Underline keywords in statements
and pay attention to scanning words.

83
00:07:58,690 --> 00:08:00,830
First, we need to find the keywords.

84
00:08:00,830 --> 00:08:07,670
The content keywords here are global rules,
environmental protection and international finances.

85
00:08:08,230 --> 00:08:11,790
Also, pay close attention to the comparison word before.

86
00:08:12,410 --> 00:08:17,030
This is crucial because the question is
asking about the timing or order of events.

87
00:08:18,450 --> 00:08:22,270
Step 2. Scan passages to locate information areas.

88
00:08:22,730 --> 00:08:24,330
Now, scan the text quickly.

89
00:08:24,910 --> 00:08:27,510
We are looking for environment and finances.

90
00:08:27,510 --> 00:08:30,330
We find these words in the first paragraph.

91
00:08:30,830 --> 00:08:37,010
The text says, these rules help manage issues
such as the environment, safety and finances.

92
00:08:38,190 --> 00:08:42,770
Step 3. Read information areas
carefully to decide the answer.

93
00:08:43,190 --> 00:08:46,690
Now, compare the statement with the text to find the writer's view.

94
00:08:47,010 --> 00:08:51,490
The statement claims environmental rules came before financial rules.

95
00:08:51,490 --> 00:08:57,430
The text says, they help manage issues such
as the environment, safety and finances.

96
00:08:58,950 --> 00:09:01,550
Does the writer say which one came first?

97
00:09:01,830 --> 00:09:02,030
No.

98
00:09:02,350 --> 00:09:06,670
The writer simply lists them as examples of what global rules manage.

99
00:09:07,110 --> 00:09:12,910
There is absolutely no information about the history
or the timeline of when these rules were created.

100
00:09:13,330 --> 00:09:16,370
Therefore, we cannot say if the statement is yes or no.

101
00:09:16,590 --> 00:09:17,810
The answer is not given.

102
00:09:18,190 --> 00:09:18,490
See?

103
00:09:18,490 --> 00:09:22,670
Even though the keywords are in the text, the specific comparison is missing.

104
00:09:22,970 --> 00:09:26,150
This is why reading carefully in step 3 is so important.

105
00:09:31,540 --> 00:09:35,560
Before finishing the lesson, we are going
to quickly recap what we learned today.

106
00:09:35,980 --> 00:09:37,600
After this lesson, we can

107
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:42,580
Understand and identify some features of yes, no or not given questions.

108
00:09:43,660 --> 00:09:49,860
Understand and identify some features of three
types of answers to yes, no or not given questions.

109
00:09:51,040 --> 00:09:54,980
Understand and apply some steps for yes, no or not given questions.

110
00:09:55,640 --> 00:09:59,050
Three steps for yes, no or not given questions are

111
00:10:00,120 --> 00:10:04,740
Step 1. Underline keywords in statements
and pay attention to scanning words.

112
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:10,880
Step 2. Scan passages to locate information
areas containing keywords and their paraphrases.

113
00:10:11,600 --> 00:10:17,780
Step 3. Read the information areas located in
step 2 carefully to identify correct answers.

114
00:10:17,780 --> 00:10:22,940
To master these steps, don't forget to do
the exercises to apply what you've learned.

115
00:10:23,500 --> 00:10:27,180
This is also the final lesson in our reading course at the intermediate level.

116
00:10:27,700 --> 00:10:31,640
I hope this course has helped you grasp the reading skills and steps for

117
00:10:31,640 --> 00:10:35,080
handling various types of questions in the IELTS reading test.

118
00:10:35,500 --> 00:10:37,020
See you in the upcoming courses.

119
00:10:37,480 --> 00:10:37,720
Goodbye!

